  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe propose a constrained optimization approach using sequential quadratic programming (SQP) with a robust initialization strategy starting from `initial_h_values`. This method efficiently handles the non-linear objective and constraints, leveraging numerical gradients for convergence. By focusing on precise constraint handling and leveraging a strong initial guess, we aim to find a lower C\u2085 value without the stochastic overhead of a genetic algorithm.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nDefine h \u2208 [0,1]^n with \u2211h_i = n/2. The overlap for shift k is \u2211h_i(1 - h_{i + k}) (circular with reflection). The objective is min over h of max_k(\u2211h_i(1 - h_{i +k})). Each term is bilinear; thus, the problem is non-convex. We pose it as a constrained optimization problem:\n\nMinimize C\nSubject to:\n- \u2211h_i = n/2\n- 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1\n- C \u2265 \u2211h_i(1 - h_{i +k}) for all k\n\nUse the initial feasible h as a starting point and apply SQP to minimize C.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Read `initial_h_values` and validate feasibility.\nb) Set up SQP problem:\n   - Decision variables: h \u2208 \u211d^n\n   - Objective: max_k \u2211h_i(1 - h_{i +k})\n   - Equality constraints: \u2211h_i = n/2\n   - Bounds: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1\nc) Compute the objective and constraints with numerical gradients.\nd) Use SLSQP solver via scipy.optimize.minimize with constraints.\ne) If initial solve fails, perturb h slightly within bounds and retry.\nf) Validate feasibility and compute C\u2085.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nUse SLSQP with:\n- Initial guess: `initial_h_values`\n- Bounds [0,1] enforced exactly\n- Equality constraint enforced with tolerance 1e-8\n- Maximum iterations: 1000\n- Tolerance: 1e-10\n\nIf no improvement, perturb h by small random values (\u00b11e-4) and rerun.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nExpected runtime dominated by optimizer iterations. SLSQP is efficient, and with a good initial guess, likely to converge in allowed time. Monitor and halt if no improvement after 1000 iterations, returning best solution.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nCheck h_i \u2208 [0,1] and sum(h) \u2248 n/2. Recompute overlap for all shifts to ensure C\u2085 is valid. Return (h, C\u2085, n_points).",
